{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>unto En Mishpat</b> — on the basis of the future name, for in the future Moses and Aaron would be judged there concerning that spring — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 14:7), from aggadah (Bereishit Rabbah 19:14). But I do not understand this, for this \"Kadesh\" is \"Kadesh Barnea,\" for it is by \"El Paran, which is upon the wilderness,\" and from it the spies were sent in the second year, as it is said (Numbers 13:26), \"unto the wilderness of Paran, to Kadesh,\" and it is written (Deuteronomy 1:19, 22), \"and we came unto Kadesh Barnea,\" \"and you said, Let us send men before us,\" and there Israel dwelt many days. But the \"Kadesh\" where the judgment of the righteous took place is in the wilderness of Zin, where they came in the fortieth year, as it is said (Numbers 20:1), \"And the children of Israel, even the whole congregation, came into the wilderness of Zin in the first month, and the people abode in Kadesh,\" and so the pericah ends. Perhaps the midrash concerns only the name — Scripture hints that this place named \"Kadesh\" would become a spring of judgment. And Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 14:7) rendered it as the plain of division of judgment, and I do not know what it means. Perhaps it is from the language of \" streams of water\" (Isaiah 30:25), \"Who split a channel for the torrent\" (Job 38:25), and similarly in the language of the Sages (Esther Rabbah 5), \"a fork (palgo) of the sea\" — he means that in that plain (mishor) was the spring of judgment and the depth of law, for it was a fine level ground suited for kings; there they would sit to judge all the nations in those lands." "<b>the field of the Amalekite</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 14:7): Amalek had not yet been born, and it is called by the future name. And I do not know whether his intent is to say that Moses our Master called the place by the name it was known by in his own days — and if so, there is nothing future about it — or what this prophecy of the nations could be, that they would prophesy and call the place accordingly. And the language of Bereishit Rabbah (Bereishit Rabbah 42:7) is: Amalek had not yet been born, and you say, \"all the field of the Amalekite\"? Rather, it declares the end from the beginning. And this is their way of derash in many places; even regarding the rivers of the Garden of Eden they said (Bereishit Rabbah 16:2) in this same manner — and their intent is to say that from the time the rivers went forth, it was stated that the river goes toward the land that would in the future belong to Assyria. But what seems correct in my eyes regarding the field of the Amalekite is that in the early days there was an eminent man from among the sons of the Horite, the inhabitant of the land, who ruled over that place, and his name was Amalek; and Eliphaz the firstborn of Esav named his son after that man — or perhaps he was from the family of Timna his mother — and he also ruled in that place and was an aluf (chieftain) over them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Onkelos's Aramaic מישר פלוג דינא is rendered here literally as 'plain of division of judgment'; Ramban himself puzzles over it and proposes a connection to the Hebrew root פלג (to split/divide) in biblical and rabbinic usage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:29:17.671447400Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}